The new wall reached once a real --part string (Strix's confirmed, Phoenix's
candidate) gets past architecture derivation -- see aiecompiler_part_strix_
confirmed.log and aiecompiler_part_phoenix_candidate.log. Both fail
identically, regardless of --target={x86sim,hw} and regardless of which
part string was used:

  adf/new_frontend/adf.h:30:10: fatal error: 'iostream' file not found

This is the ADF C++ frontend's own preprocessing pass (invoked internally
by aiecompiler via Peano's bundled clang, before any AIE-target codegen
happens) failing to find a host C++ standard library. It is independent of
the AIE array entirely -- it would block a Strix build exactly the same way
it blocks a Phoenix one.

Checked whether this SDK ships a C++ stdlib anywhere for Peano to find:

  find <ryzen-ai-1.7.1 env> -iname iostream   ->   no results

Checked whether Visual Studio (MSVC's STL, the other place a Windows
install would normally get <iostream> from) is present on this machine at
all:

  no Microsoft Visual Studio / Build Tools install found under either
  Program Files location; no vcvarsall.bat anywhere under Program Files

Conclusion: this is a genuine, structural gap in this pip-packaged
distribution -- Peano is shipped for AIE-target code generation, but
nothing shipped alongside it, and nothing installed on this machine,
supplies the host-side C++ standard library its own ADF frontend needs
just to preprocess a graph description. Unlike the --part wall (which
turned out to be solvable from data already on disk), this one is not:
either install a full C++ toolchain (Visual Studio Build Tools, at minimum
its C++ workload) on this machine and point Peano's clang at its include
path, or find a machine that already has one.

Not attempted: installing Visual Studio Build Tools was judged out of
scope for this pass -- a multi-GB software install is a real, standing
change to this shared machine, not a quick check, and belongs in its own
explicitly-approved step rather than folded into this investigation.
